What is a Process in Linux?

A process in Linux is simply a program in execution. Whenever you run a command, script, or open an application, Linux creates a process to manage that execution. Each process has its own memory, CPU time, and system resources.

Think of it like this:
The program is a recipe.
The process is the actual cooking happening in the kitchen.
Multiple people can cook the same recipe (i.e., run the same program), but each cooking session is separate — just like each process.

Important Points:
Every process has a unique ID called a PID (Process ID).
It also has a parent process, which is the one that started it — this parent has its own PID, known as the PPID.
A foreground process is one you interact with directly in the terminal.
A background process runs behind the scenes — you don’t see its output unless you check logs or monitor it.
A process uses system resources: CPU, memory, disk, etc., and ends when its task is complete.



Common Commands:
ps shows a list of currently running processes.
top gives a live, real-time view of CPU and memory usage by processes.
kill allows you to stop a process by its PID.
You can run processes in the background using &, and bring them to foreground using fg.

Real-world Scenario:
Imagine your Jenkins job is stuck. You log in to the Linux server, use ps or top to find the Jenkins process, and use kill <PID> to terminate it safely — that’s you managing processes like a DevOps pro.


Final Tip:
Don’t just memorize commands — understand how Linux handles processes. It’s the foundation for debugging, performance tuning, and automation.
